{
 "allowed_use": "Evidence that realised grouped-program operand reuse depends on dispatch proximity, and that the shared miss core's lockstep-by-K order misprices it bidirectionally across two L2 capacities.",
 "counters": [
  "dram__bytes_read.sum",
  "l1tex__m_xbar2l1tex_read_bytes.sum"
 ],
 "finding": "Realised operand reuse in a grouped matrix program depends on how close in dispatch order the programs that reference one object are: holding every other quantity fixed, measured downstream read falls monotonically as the group width rises from 1 to 16 on both targets (A800 279.6->162.4 MiB, C500 483.8->196.3 MiB against 160.0 MiB of compulsory traffic). The shared miss core's lockstep-by-K wave order misprices this in opposite directions on the two capacities: on the 40 MiB target it returns exactly compulsory traffic at every group width and so under-predicts by up to 1.75x, while on the 8 MiB target it over-responds and over-predicts by up to 2.80x. Aliasing every expert onto one weight buffer holds the reference order fixed and changes only the distinct footprint; it does not close the gap but widens the ratio, so the effect is not footprint against capacity. Shortening tiles from 64 to 26 rows leaves the ratio flat, so it is not drift between unequal programs. At group width 8 the core is within 7 percent on C500, which is why this mechanism does not account for the production grouped-MoE shortfall at that group width.",
 "forbidden_use": "Any duration. The C500 layer-1 per-kernel pass replays kernels and its reported time is not execution time; nothing here may price a Forward path. Also not an L2 capacity, a DRAM bandwidth, a replacement policy, or a per-shape traffic correction.",
